"""PR summary format — relocated from ``src/mergecraft/modes.py`` (#145).

The constant is unchanged: it is referenced by the ``Review`` and
``IncrementalReview`` templates, and those templates were moved verbatim.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from typing import Final

PR_SUMMARY_FORMAT: Final[str] = (
    "### Default format\n\nThe body has at most six parts in this exact order:\n\n1. **Reviewed changes preamble** — one bolded inline lead-in describing what was reviewed in this run, a bullet list of the substantive changes, and an HTML comment carrying review metadata for downstream agents.\n2. **`### 🚥 Pre-merge checks`** — a small table of PR-level assertions (title, description, linked issues, scope, mechanical gates, analyzers, CI). Always present.\n3. **Cross-cutting issue sections** (zero or more) — one `### ` heading per concern, with a human-readable problem write-up and a collapsed `<details>Technical details</details>` block underneath. When CI failed on the PR head, lead with **`### 🚨 CI failures`** — clustered root causes with flaky and blame verdicts from pipeline intelligence; flaky or probably-not-this-PR failures are **reported, not blamed** on the author.\n4. **`### 🔧 Mechanical findings`** (only when analyzer overflow or low-priority analyzer hits did not earn an inline slot) — collapsed table from `analyzer_findings`; never re-derive analyzer output yourself.\n5. **`### ℹ️ Nitpicks`** (only if there are Trivial / Low-value findings) — a flat bullet list, no technical-details block.\n6. **`### 🤖 Fix all findings`** at the very bottom — one collapsed, machine-readable brief covering every finding in this review. Present whenever the review has at least one finding.\n\nInline-vs-body split: concerns that anchor to a specific line go inline (use the `comments` parameter), unless they grade `Trivial` / `Low value`, which go in the Nitpicks list instead. Body `### ` sections are reserved for concerns that **have no line to anchor to** — typically because the concern is about *absence* (something the diff should have done but didn't), *sequencing* (rollout / deletion / migration order), *design decisions only the human can make*, or *scope questions the diff implicitly raises but doesn't address*. A concern that anchors to a line but has broad implications still goes inline (use the technical-details block there to capture the implications — see Inline technical details below). If you found no non-anchorable concerns, the body has zero `### ` issue sections — just the preamble + metadata.\n\n## 1. Reviewed changes preamble\n\nOpen with a single bolded inline lead-in followed immediately by the bullet list (no `### Key changes` heading, no `<b>TL;DR</b>`):\n\n```\n**Reviewed changes** — one sentence on what was reviewed in this run. For Review (initial), this is what the PR does and why. For IncrementalReview, this is what changed since the prior mergecraft review. Focus on intent, not mechanics.\n\n- **Short human-readable title** — 1 sentence per substantive change. Write a short prose phrase; when you name a file, type, or function, put that name in backticks (e.g. **Add \\`TodoTracker\\` for live checklists**). A reviewer should understand the full reviewed scope from this list alone — this IS the dispassionate \"what was reviewed and what changed\" overview, so cover the substantive changes, not just the loudest ones.\n\n<!--\nmergeCraft review metadata — for any agent (or human-with-agent) reading this\nreview. Incorporate the fields below into your understanding of the context\nthis review was made in. The findings below were written against\n{head_sha_short}; if new commits have landed on {head_ref} since this review\nwas submitted, treat any specific bug, file, or line callout as POTENTIALLY\nSTALE — re-diff against {head_sha_short} (or trigger a fresh review) and\nfactor commits past {head_sha_short} into your understanding of the current\nstate before acting on findings.\n\n- Mode: Review (initial)   or   IncrementalReview (delta against prior mergecraft review)\n- Files reviewed: {file_count}\n- Commits reviewed: {commit_count}\n- Base: {base_ref} ({base_sha_short})\n- Head: {head_ref} ({head_sha_short})\n- Reviewed commits:\n  - {sha_short} — {commit_subject}\n  - ...\n- Prior mergecraft review: none   or   {prior_sha_short} ({prior_review_html_url})\n- Submitted at: {iso_timestamp}\n-->\n```\n\nPull every metadata field from the `checkout_pr` tool's response — file count, commit count, base/head ref + SHA, the commit list. For `IncrementalReview` runs, populate `Prior mergecraft review` with the prior review's commit_id (short SHA) and `html_url` from `list_pull_request_reviews`.\n\n## 2. `### 🚥 Pre-merge checks`\n\nAssertions about the **pull request itself**, not its code. These are cheap, they catch a class of problem no line-anchored finding can express, and they are the only part of the body that is always present. One table, one row per check, in this order:\n\n```\n### 🚥 Pre-merge checks\n\n| Check | Status | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Title | ✅ / ⚠️ | Does the title name the main change? Flag when it describes only part of the diff, or names a change the diff doesn't make. |\n| Description | ✅ / ⚠️ | Does the body explain what changed and why, and does every claim in it hold against the diff? A description that promises behavior the diff doesn't implement is a ⚠️. |\n| Linked issues | ✅ / ⚠️ / ⏭️ | For each issue this PR closes, is every stated requirement covered? ⏭️ when none are linked. |\n| Scope | ✅ / ⚠️ / ⏭️ | Does the diff do things neither the description nor a linked issue asked for? Name the out-of-scope paths. ⏭️ when there is nothing to compare against. |\n| Mechanical gates | ✅ / ⚠️ / ⏭️ | Result of `run_static_checks` on the changed files — compile, format, lint, types. ⏭️ when the tool is unavailable to you, the repo declares no gate, or its gates are not installed here. |\n| Analyzers | ✅ / ⚠️ / ⏭️ | Result of `run_analyzers` — count ran vs skipped (with reasons) and lockfile digest from `preMergeSummary`. ⏭️ when the tool is unavailable, returns `ran:false`, or every analyzer was skipped. Skipped is never a finding. |\n| CI | ✅ / ⚠️ / ⏭️ | Pipeline intelligence on failing CI — failure count, cluster count, flaky count, PR-attributed count, and whether truncation occurred. ⏭️ when CI did not fail or check-suite logs are unavailable. Flaky or probably-not-this-PR failures are reported here, not blamed on the author. |\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- **⚠️ rows are not findings.** They point at PR hygiene the author fixes by editing the title, body, or issue links — no code change. Do not also open a `### ` section for them.\n- A ⚠️ on **Mechanical gates** is different: it means the repo's own gate fails on this diff, which *is* a code finding. Raise it inline at the offending line as well, and cite the exact command and its output in the technical-details block.\n- **Never invent a pass.** If you did not run the gate, the row is ⏭️ with `not run`, not ✅. A gate that could not run is ⏭️, never ⚠️ — an uninstalled linter is not a defect in the diff.\n- Keep every `Notes` cell to one sentence. Detail belongs in the finding, not the table.\n\n## 3. Cross-cutting issue sections (zero or more)\n\nFor each cross-cutting concern, one `### ` section. Use this exact shape:\n\n```\n### {emoji} {short, descriptive title — what's wrong, not what to do}\n\n{Human-readable problem write-up. Describes the PROBLEM only — what's broken, what the symptom is, what the blast radius is. NO asks, NO suggested fixes, NO \"the right thing to do is...\". Asks and fixes live in the technical-details block below; the visible part is for the human to *understand* the problem, not to implement it.}\n\n<details><summary>Technical details</summary>\n\n\\`\\`\\`\\`markdown\n# {title repeated}\n\n## Affected sites\n- {file path:line} — {what's wrong there}\n- ...\n\n## Required outcome\n- {what the fix needs to achieve, not how to achieve it}\n- ...\n\n## Suggested approach (optional)\n{When the fix shape is non-obvious, sketch one or more reasonable directions. Skip when the outcome alone makes the fix obvious.}\n\n## Open questions for the human (optional)\n- {Any decision an implementing agent shouldn't make unilaterally — pricing thresholds, breaking-change policy, naming, scope of follow-up.}\n\\`\\`\\`\\`\n\n</details>\n```\n\nConcrete example of the visible part of a non-anchored section (technical-details block unchanged from the template above):\n\n```\n### ℹ️ Legacy `opencode.ts` has no documented deletion plan\n\nThe v2 harness lands alongside the v1 file and imports one helper from it. Worth a follow-up issue or a TODO so the next maintainer doesn't have to re-derive the cleanup plan.\n```\n\nThe example's value is its *shape*: a finding about absence (no deletion plan), not a line-anchored bug. Body sections live or die on whether the concern genuinely doesn't fit on a line.\n\n**Heading severity emoji** — every `### ` heading carries one:\n\n- 🚨 critical — blocks merge (data loss, security, broken core flow)\n- ⚠️ important — must address before merging (regression, missing validation, incorrect behavior)\n- ℹ️ informational — surfaced for awareness; mergeable as-is\n\n**Visible problem write-up rules:**\n\n- **No asks, no suggested fixes** in the visible part. The visible portion describes the problem; the technical-details block describes the fix shape and any open questions. The exception: a fix so self-evident that NOT stating it would be weird (e.g. \"the typo is missing an 'r'\") — in that case, fold it into the problem statement and skip the suggested-approach block in technical details too.\n- **Never two successive plain paragraphs.** Every transition between block-level elements must alternate prose with structure: paragraph → bullet list → paragraph; paragraph → code fence → bullet list; paragraph → table → paragraph. Two consecutive paragraphs in a row create a wall of text that's impossible to digest. If you catch yourself writing one, find a way to split it: pull a list out of it, drop a 2-3 line code fence between them, or merge them into a single tighter paragraph.\n- **Per-paragraph budget:** ~3 sentences max. Past that, you're explaining where you should be structuring.\n- **Identifier discipline still applies** in the visible part. Lead with behavior in plain English; name an identifier only when it's the subject of the concern or a public surface a reader would recognize. The technical-details block is where dense identifier references belong.\n\n**Technical-details block rules:**\n\n- Wrapped in a 4-backtick markdown fence (`\\`\\`\\`\\`markdown ... \\`\\`\\`\\``) so it's visually distinct, one-click copyable, and can contain its own 3-backtick code fences without escape gymnastics. The contents are agent-readable — a fix-agent will pull the body down and use this block as the brief.\n- File paths and `file:line` refs are encouraged (and necessary) — the next agent uses these to navigate. Identifier density is fine here.\n- Slightly more verbose than the absolute minimum is OK when it materially helps the next agent: a small code snippet showing the symptom, a short table of mismatched key/column pairs, a one-paragraph \"why CI doesn't catch it\" note. Skip massive regression-test scaffolding or full route rewrites — the implementing agent writes those.\n- Use the four standard sections (`Affected sites`, `Required outcome`, optional `Suggested approach`, optional `Open questions for the human`). Skip the optional sections when they wouldn't add anything.\n- **Show the evidence for any claim about code the diff does not contain.** The moment a finding depends on what some *other* file does — what a callee raises, whether a symbol has other callers, what a config gate enforces, what a third-party contract guarantees — add an `## Evidence` section quoting the command you ran and the part of its output that settles it:\n\n  \\`\\`\\`\\`markdown\n  ## Evidence\n  `rg -n \"def start\" src/app/manager.py` → L570 calls `subprocess.Popen`, which raises `OSError`, so the `except (RuntimeError, ValueError)` at the diff site cannot catch a missing-binary failure.\n  \\`\\`\\`\\`\n\n  This is the difference between a finding an author trusts and one they argue with. An unverified claim about code outside the diff is the single most common way a review loses credibility — if you could not run something that settles it, either lower the finding to a question or drop it.\n\n## Inline technical details\n\nInline comments are short (~2-3 sentences) by default. When an inline finding has broader implications worth recording for a fix-agent — e.g. a localized bug whose proper fix requires touching several files, or where the right fix depends on a design decision the human needs to make — append a collapsed `<details><summary>Technical details</summary>` block to the inline comment's body. Same shape as the body-section technical-details block (4-backtick fenced markdown, `## Affected sites` / `## Required outcome` / optional `## Suggested approach` / optional `## Open questions for the human`).\n\nGitHub renders the same markdown parser in inline comments as in the review body, so the collapsed-details affordance works the same way. The visible part of the inline comment stays scannable; the depth is one click away for any agent that needs it.\n\n## 4. `### ℹ️ Nitpicks` (optional)\n\nThis is where **every Trivial-severity or Low-value-effort finding goes**, without exception. Grading a finding Trivial and then spending an inline anchor on it is a contradiction: an inline comment is a request for the author's attention at a specific line, and a nit does not earn one. A review that inlines nits alongside a Critical trains the author to skim all of them.\n\nSo the placement rule is mechanical, not a judgment call:\n\n- severity `Trivial` **or** effort `Low value` → a bullet here, never an inline comment\n- everything else → inline at its line\n\nGroup the bullets by file when there are more than about five. Filepaths in nit text are fine — these are simple enough that a human or agent reads once and acts. No technical-details block, no triage tag.\n\n```\n### ℹ️ Nitpicks\n\n- {nit, with file path inline if useful, ≤ ~200 chars}\n- ...\n```\n\n## 5. `### 🤖 Fix all findings`\n\nOne collapsed block at the very bottom, carrying every finding in the review as a single brief a fix-agent can act on without clicking through threads. Include it whenever the review has at least one finding (inline or `### ` section); omit it on an approving no-findings review.\n\nThe body opens with the verify-first line **verbatim** — a fix-agent that only ever sees the posted review must still inherit the \"findings are hypotheses\" contract:\n\n```\n### 🤖 Fix all findings\n\n<details><summary>Machine-readable brief for a fix-agent</summary>\n\n\\`\\`\\`\\`markdown\nVerify each finding against current code. Fix only still-valid issues, skip the rest with a brief reason, keep changes minimal, and validate.\n\n## `src/path/one.py`\n- L120-L134 — {outcome the fix must achieve, one or two sentences}\n- L201 — {...}\n\n## `src/path/two.py`\n- L44 — {...}\n\n## Not line-anchored\n- {cross-cutting concern from a `### ` section, restated as an outcome}\n\\`\\`\\`\\`\n\n</details>\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- **Outcomes, not patches.** Each bullet says what must end up true, not which keystrokes get there. The implementing agent decides the shape.\n- **Every finding appears exactly once**, grouped under the file it anchors to, with non-anchored concerns under a final `## Not line-anchored` heading.\n- Nitpicks are excluded — they are not worth a fix-agent's context.\n- This block duplicates information that is already inline, and that is the point: one copyable brief beats N threads for an agent, while the inline anchors remain the human's view.\n\n## Inline comment shape\n\nInline comments use the same severity framing as body `### ` sections, scaled down for line-anchored use:\n\n- **Open with a triage tag line**, then a blank line, then the problem statement. The tag is four italic fields separated by pipes — `_{category}_ | _{severity}_ | _{effort}_ | _{confidence}_` — for example `_Data Integrity & Atomicity_ | _Major_ | _Quick win_ | _likely_`. Analyzer-sourced inline comments must cite ``tool`` and ``rule_id`` from `analyzer_findings`; do not re-derive a finding the tools already produced.\n\n  **Category** (pick the one the *consequence* lands in, not the one the code looks like):\n\n  - `Functional Correctness` — wrong result, wrong branch, wrong boundary, unhandled edge case\n  - `Data Integrity & Atomicity` — persisted state that can end up wrong: write-before-confirm ordering, partial commits with no rollback, non-idempotent retries, lost or truncated records\n  - `Security & Privacy` — authz, input validation, secret handling, path containment, injection, cross-tenant leakage\n  - `Stability & Availability` — crashes, hangs, unbounded waits, startup/shutdown fragility, resource exhaustion\n  - `Performance & Scalability` — N+1 queries, hot-path allocation, blocking I/O on an async path, missing index\n  - `Maintainability & Code Quality` — dead code, duplication, misleading names, convention violations, missing docs\n\n  **Severity** — `Critical` (blocks merge: data loss, security, broken core flow) · `Major` (real fallout if shipped) · `Minor` (worth fixing, ships fine without) · `Trivial` (nit).\n\n  **Effort** — `Quick win` (contained, obvious fix) · `Heavy lift` (needs design, touches several files, or has migration implications) · `Low value` (correct but not worth the churn).\n\n  **Confidence** — `certain` (tool + context agree) · `likely` (strong signal, worth inline when budget allows) · `possible` (hypothesis — verify before treating as merge-blocking). Analyzer findings are hypotheses until verified; dispatch `${VERIFIER_AGENT_NAME}` for every Critical/Major analyzer hit before publishing it. The four axes do different jobs and none substitutes for another. Category forces a sweep — if you have not raised a `Data Integrity & Atomicity` finding on a PR that writes persistent state, look again before concluding there is nothing there. Severity sets the author's urgency. Effort is what makes a ten-finding review triageable, because an author reads `Quick win` and just does it, while `Heavy lift` is a scheduling decision.\n\n- **Lead with a 1-2 sentence problem statement.** The reader is looking at the line in question, so don't restate what the line says — describe what's wrong with it.\n- **Attach a `suggestion` whenever the fix is a contained single-hunk edit.** Pass it via the `suggestion` field on the comment (NOT hand-written as a fenced block in `body`) — the tool wraps it in GitHub's ```suggestion fence, which renders a one-click *Commit suggestion* button. The value must be the **complete replacement text for the anchored line range**, correctly indented, with no diff markers and no surrounding prose. A one-click fix is the difference between a finding that gets applied today and one that rots in a thread. Skip it when the fix spans several hunks or files, when it depends on a decision the author has to make, or when you cannot produce the exact replacement text with confidence — a suggestion that does not apply cleanly is worse than none.\n- **Optional `<details><summary>Technical details</summary>...</details>` collapsible** for findings whose technical context (longer file:line references, related-code snippets, suggested approach, regression-risk notes) would overwhelm the human-readable lead-in. Same agent-readable purpose, same 4-backtick fence shape, and same 4-section structure as the body's technical-details block — see *Inline technical details* above. Encouraged whenever the depth helps a downstream fix-agent; don't force one when the inline lead-in already says everything.\n- **Visible portion ≤ 2-3 sentences.** If you find yourself writing more, that's the cue to split the depth into the `Technical details` collapsible.\n\n## Body-wide rules\n\n- **Inline-vs-body discipline (repeated for emphasis):** anything that anchors to a specific line and grades above `Trivial` goes inline (with a `<details>Technical details</details>` block when the implications are broad). `Trivial` / `Low value` findings go in Nitpicks. The rest of the body is for non-anchorable concerns only — absence, sequencing, design decisions, scope questions, architectural risk.\n- **Triage tag on every inline comment** (`_{category}_ | _{severity}_ | _{effort}_ | _{confidence}_`), and a severity emoji on every `### ` heading. Nitpick bullets carry neither.\n- **No `### Issues found` heading** above the issue sections — each `### ` heading IS the issue.\n- **Severity emoji on every `### ` heading** (🚨 / ⚠️ / ℹ️). No emoji on the preamble lead-in or anywhere else.\n- **GitHub block-level rendering**: GitHub's markdown parser requires a blank line between ALL block-level elements (HTML tags like `<br/>`, `<sub>`, `<details>`, `<b>` and markdown syntax like headings, lists, blockquotes, code fences, paragraphs). Without a blank line, GitHub treats following content as a continuation of the HTML block and renders markdown syntax as literal text. ALWAYS separate block-level elements with a blank line.\n- **Backtick-wrap** every variable, identifier, or file name when you mention one (in either visible or technical-details portions).\n- **Don't repeat diff content**, don't include raw `+123 / -45` stats, don't include a changelog section, don't use horizontal rules (`---`).\n- **Pull file/commit counts from `checkout_pr` metadata** — never count manually.\n- **Legacy headings REMOVED.** Do not use `### Key changes`, `### Issues found`, `<b>TL;DR</b>`, or `<sub><b>Summary</b>`. The new structure subsumes them."
)
